WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.285
Hello. In about sixteen minutes, I will teach you the whole AI ethics course,

2
00:00:05.285 --> 00:00:07.200
the way you need it for the exam.

3
00:00:07.550 --> 00:00:13.412
Not every detail. The ideas that come back again and again, how they connect,

4
00:00:13.412 --> 00:00:14.900
and how to turn them into answers.

5
00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:20.806
The course has five lectures. The first two, by Tiphaine Viard,

6
00:00:20.806 --> 00:00:25.675
look at AI as a sociotechnical system, and at the landscape of AI ethics.

7
00:00:26.375 --> 00:00:33.419
The third, by Thomas Le Goff, is about regulation: soft law, the GDPR, and the AI Act.

8
00:00:33.419 --> 00:00:38.125
The fourth, by Mathilde Abel, is about the human labour behind AI.

9
00:00:38.825 --> 00:00:45.444
The fifth comes back to users: bias, technosolutionism, how people really use algorithms,

10
00:00:45.444 --> 00:00:46.975
and how they can contest them.

11
00:00:47.675 --> 00:00:53.300
If you remember only one thing, remember three questions you can ask of any AI system.

12
00:00:53.650 --> 00:00:59.018
Where does the system start and end? Who does the work, who decides, and who is affected?

13
00:00:59.018 --> 00:01:01.925
And which rule applies, with which concrete safeguard?

14
00:01:02.275 --> 00:01:05.950
Every exam question is a variation on these three.

15
00:01:07.050 --> 00:01:10.968
We start with the most important idea of the course: framing.

16
00:01:10.968 --> 00:01:14.600
The teachers said there will be at least one framing question.

17
00:01:14.950 --> 00:01:21.359
Look at the left. If you frame a credit-scoring system as just a model, the only question is:

18
00:01:21.359 --> 00:01:22.175
is the score accurate?

19
00:01:22.875 --> 00:01:27.185
Now look at the right. A sociotechnical framing includes the data,

20
00:01:27.185 --> 00:01:32.605
the annotators who labelled it, the advisers who use the score, the bank’s targets,

21
00:01:32.605 --> 00:01:36.500
the regulators, the applicants, and even the energy and hardware.

22
00:01:36.850 --> 00:01:42.175
Now the question becomes: who gets access to credit, and who bears the errors?

23
00:01:42.875 --> 00:01:46.884
So framing means deciding where the object starts and ends.

24
00:01:46.884 --> 00:01:50.000
Every framing shows some problems and hides others.

25
00:01:50.350 --> 00:01:56.250
In an exam answer, always say which framing you choose, and what a narrower framing would hide.

26
00:01:57.350 --> 00:02:02.850
How do we study a sociotechnical system? Three methods are enough for a short question.

27
00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:08.945
Ethnography and interviews, to see what people actually do. Discourse analysis,

28
00:02:08.945 --> 00:02:14.689
to study the words in charters, newspapers and laws. And computational methods,

29
00:02:14.689 --> 00:02:16.825
like network maps of scientific papers.

30
00:02:17.525 --> 00:02:20.764
The history of AI teaches the same lesson.

31
00:02:20.764 --> 00:02:25.082
Symbolic AI encodes explicit rules and expert knowledge.

32
00:02:25.082 --> 00:02:30.634
Connectionist AI learns statistical patterns from large amounts of data,

33
00:02:30.634 --> 00:02:32.325
and sacrifices intelligibility.

34
00:02:33.025 --> 00:02:38.646
The common myth is that neural AI simply replaced symbolic AI. In fact,

35
00:02:38.646 --> 00:02:40.275
they coexist and compete.

36
00:02:40.625 --> 00:02:47.600
Defining AI has always been a fight for scientific space. That is why AI is a polysemic word.

37
00:02:48.700 --> 00:02:54.275
Next, materiality. The word cloud makes AI sound immaterial. It is not.

38
00:02:54.625 --> 00:03:00.186
It depends on minerals like lithium and rare earths, on water, and on energy,

39
00:03:00.186 --> 00:03:02.375
for training and for every single query.

40
00:03:03.075 --> 00:03:07.225
And it depends on human labour, which we will see in lecture four.

41
00:03:07.925 --> 00:03:10.948
Two concepts about power. Data colonialism:

42
00:03:10.948 --> 00:03:15.517
value is extracted from data and concentrated in a few companies.

43
00:03:15.517 --> 00:03:20.650
A community gives its language data, and it is sold back to them as a service.

44
00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:27.480
Reactivity: people adapt to what is measured. When a ranking counts international students,

45
00:03:27.480 --> 00:03:29.700
universities recruit to raise the number.

46
00:03:30.400 --> 00:03:36.992
So, following Kalluri, don’t only ask whether an AI is fair. Ask who gains power,

47
00:03:36.992 --> 00:03:37.492
and who loses it.

48
00:03:38.375 --> 00:03:44.325
Lecture two looks at AI ethics itself. Jobin and colleagues read eighty-four guidelines.

49
00:03:44.675 --> 00:03:51.353
They converge on five principles: transparency, justice and fairness, non-maleficence,

50
00:03:51.353 --> 00:03:52.750
responsibility and privacy.

51
00:03:53.450 --> 00:03:57.683
But the same words mean different things to different actors.

52
00:03:57.683 --> 00:04:02.401
And the charters organise around three poles: technical, regulatory,

53
00:04:02.401 --> 00:04:03.575
and business or innovation.

54
00:04:04.275 --> 00:04:11.155
The big weakness is the operationalisation gap. Principles rarely become concrete procedures.

55
00:04:11.155 --> 00:04:14.450
And some topics are missing: work, power, and ecology.

56
00:04:15.150 --> 00:04:21.252
Legally, charters are soft law. They are not binding, they can bypass democratic debate,

57
00:04:21.252 --> 00:04:23.675
and they can be used for ethics washing.

58
00:04:24.025 --> 00:04:30.730
But soft law can harden: when a company publicly commits, or when the law absorbs it,

59
00:04:30.730 --> 00:04:31.230
as the AI Act did.

60
00:04:32.150 --> 00:04:37.575
Who defines what AI risk is? Benbouzid and colleagues describe four arenas.

61
00:04:37.925 --> 00:04:41.835
The super-intelligence arena fears loss of control.

62
00:04:41.835 --> 00:04:46.425
The open-world agents arena wants experts to certify technical risks.

63
00:04:47.125 --> 00:04:53.976
The sociotechnical criticism arena says AI reinforces power and carries systematic bias.

64
00:04:53.976 --> 00:05:00.282
And the technical-economic arena builds market conformity: standards, the AI Act,

65
00:05:00.282 --> 00:05:01.450
and the Brussels effect.

66
00:05:02.150 --> 00:05:09.645
Remember the contrast between AI safety and AI ethics. AI safety focuses on existential risk,

67
00:05:09.645 --> 00:05:14.050
is led by tech leaders, and compares AI to nuclear weapons.

68
00:05:14.400 --> 00:05:17.576
AI ethics focuses on present, unequal harms.

69
00:05:17.576 --> 00:05:22.350
The critique of the safety arena is that it distracts from those harms.

70
00:05:22.700 --> 00:05:26.900
In a government report question, these arenas are your structure.

71
00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:34.218
Lecture three: the law. For the GDPR, remember one formula: one legal basis,

72
00:05:34.218 --> 00:05:35.400
and all the principles.

73
00:05:36.100 --> 00:05:41.423
Two classic points. Pseudonymised data, where names are replaced by codes,

74
00:05:41.423 --> 00:05:42.800
are still personal data.

75
00:05:43.150 --> 00:05:50.864
Article 22 gives a right not to be subject to a solely automated decision with significant effects,

76
00:05:50.864 --> 00:05:51.800
like refusing a loan.

77
00:05:52.500 --> 00:05:59.175
Now the exam trap the teacher warned about. A student writes: a human signs the decision,

78
00:05:59.175 --> 00:06:00.725
so it is not automated. Wrong.

79
00:06:01.425 --> 00:06:05.474
In the SCHUFA case, in 2023, the Court of Justice said:

80
00:06:05.474 --> 00:06:10.350
if the human relies decisively on the score, the score is the decision.

81
00:06:10.700 --> 00:06:16.400
So Article 22 applies. Human oversight must be real, not a rubber stamp.

82
00:06:17.500 --> 00:06:20.900
For the AI Act, ask four questions in order.

83
00:06:21.250 --> 00:06:25.781
First, is it an AI system? The key criterion is inference:

84
00:06:25.781 --> 00:06:28.675
the system infers its outputs from its inputs.

85
00:06:29.375 --> 00:06:34.524
Second, which risk level? Prohibited, high-risk, transparency, or minimal,

86
00:06:34.524 --> 00:06:37.375
plus special rules for general-purpose models.

87
00:06:37.725 --> 00:06:43.820
Prohibited practices include social scoring, emotion recognition at work or school,

88
00:06:43.820 --> 00:06:45.950
untargeted scraping of faces,

89
00:06:45.950 --> 00:06:51.311
and real-time remote biometric identification by police in public spaces,

90
00:06:51.311 --> 00:06:52.650
with narrow exceptions.

91
00:06:53.000 --> 00:06:58.942
High-risk areas are listed in Annex III: biometrics, education, employment,

92
00:06:58.942 --> 00:07:03.550
credit and public benefits, law enforcement, migration and justice.

93
00:07:04.250 --> 00:07:09.813
Third, which role? The provider places the system on the market under its name.

94
00:07:09.813 --> 00:07:12.000
The deployer uses it under its authority.

95
00:07:12.700 --> 00:07:18.439
Fourth, which duties? Risk management, data governance, documentation, logs,

96
00:07:18.439 --> 00:07:22.442
human oversight and robustness for high-risk systems.

97
00:07:22.442 --> 00:07:26.175
Public deployers also do a fundamental-rights impact assessment.

98
00:07:27.275 --> 00:07:33.868
Generative AI has its own rules. A general-purpose model provider must document the model,

99
00:07:33.868 --> 00:07:37.750
inform downstream providers, have a copyright policy,

100
00:07:37.750 --> 00:07:40.200
and publish a summary of its training data.

101
00:07:40.900 --> 00:07:46.784
Above ten to the power twenty-five operations of training compute, or if designated,

102
00:07:46.784 --> 00:07:51.758
the model is presumed to have systemic risk. Then it needs red-teaming,

103
00:07:51.758 --> 00:07:54.000
incident reporting and cybersecurity.

104
00:07:54.350 --> 00:07:58.661
And open-weight models are only exempt from documentation.

105
00:07:58.661 --> 00:08:01.975
The copyright policy and training summary still apply.

106
00:08:02.675 --> 00:08:06.946
Copyright protects original human expression. In the EU,

107
00:08:06.946 --> 00:08:13.124
text-and-data mining is allowed under exceptions, but rights holders can opt out.

108
00:08:13.124 --> 00:08:15.475
The US uses fair use, with four factors.

109
00:08:16.175 --> 00:08:21.897
Finally, liability. When Air Canada’s chatbot invented a refund policy,

110
00:08:21.897 --> 00:08:26.600
the company was held to it. A disclaimer does not remove responsibility.

111
00:08:27.700 --> 00:08:34.369
Lecture four, by Mathilde Abel, asks: who makes automatic AI? A platform matches people,

112
00:08:34.369 --> 00:08:39.050
captures data, and breaks work into small tasks. That is taskification.

113
00:08:39.750 --> 00:08:45.940
Tubaro, Casilli and Coville describe three roles. The trainer works before the model:

114
00:08:45.940 --> 00:08:47.425
generating and labelling data.

115
00:08:48.125 --> 00:08:54.224
The imitator works instead of the model: humans secretly doing what is sold as AI,

116
00:08:54.224 --> 00:08:59.325
like Google Duplex calls, or theft detection watched by workers in Madagascar.

117
00:09:00.025 --> 00:09:05.775
The verifier works after the model: checking and correcting live outputs.

118
00:09:05.775 --> 00:09:07.275
And verification never goes away.

119
00:09:08.375 --> 00:09:14.287
Here is a value chain of annotation. A French start-up buys data from a platform,

120
00:09:14.287 --> 00:09:17.675
which needs ninety-five to ninety-nine percent accuracy.

121
00:09:18.375 --> 00:09:23.397
The work goes through subcontractors, black boxes within black boxes,

122
00:09:23.397 --> 00:09:29.175
down to annotators in Madagascar, paid around half a dollar to under two dollars an hour.

123
00:09:29.875 --> 00:09:33.896
Several layers of quality control check and reject the work,

124
00:09:33.896 --> 00:09:36.025
and the data goes back to the client.

125
00:09:36.375 --> 00:09:42.965
Remember: a piece rate is not an hourly wage, because searching and rejected work are unpaid.

126
00:09:42.965 --> 00:09:47.650
And the instructions, interface and metrics decide what counts as the truth.

127
00:09:48.350 --> 00:09:53.726
The teacher’s rule: never just write human in the loop. Say who these people are,

128
00:09:53.726 --> 00:09:58.400
which contract they have, how they are paid, and whether they are internal or external.

129
00:09:59.500 --> 00:10:06.208
Lecture five. The flagship bias case is COMPAS, a recidivism score with unequal errors.

130
00:10:06.208 --> 00:10:09.825
Choosing a fairness metric is itself a normative choice.

131
00:10:10.525 --> 00:10:16.017
But cases like SyRI in the Netherlands, or the French family-benefits agency,

132
00:10:16.017 --> 00:10:21.450
raise a deeper question: is a fair algorithm enough, if the practice itself is unjust?

133
00:10:22.150 --> 00:10:27.183
The mulching proposal is a satire. It makes an absurd algorithm fair,

134
00:10:27.183 --> 00:10:28.875
accountable and transparent.

135
00:10:29.225 --> 00:10:33.212
The lesson: without questioning the purpose, fairness,

136
00:10:33.212 --> 00:10:36.425
accountability and transparency can justify anything.

137
00:10:37.125 --> 00:10:42.773
That is technosolutionism: assuming technology must be part of the solution.

138
00:10:42.773 --> 00:10:48.273
Watch for the lobby lines: others will do it, regulation kills innovation,

139
00:10:48.273 --> 00:10:49.625
and the next version will fix it.

140
00:10:50.725 --> 00:10:55.950
How do people really use algorithms? Christin studied judges and web journalists.

141
00:10:56.300 --> 00:11:02.774
She found decoupling: managers say the tool is deployed, while staff ignore or resist it.

142
00:11:02.774 --> 00:11:06.075
And buffering: foot-dragging, gaming and open critique.

143
00:11:06.775 --> 00:11:09.769
The two expert fields react differently.

144
00:11:09.769 --> 00:11:14.858
Judges have high entry barriers and tie decisions to their identity.

145
00:11:14.858 --> 00:11:17.825
Journalists work in an open, profit-driven field.

146
00:11:18.525 --> 00:11:24.620
And at the French Cour de cassation, the anonymisation AI needs a permanent team.

147
00:11:24.620 --> 00:11:25.425
Training never ends.

148
00:11:26.125 --> 00:11:31.969
So adoption is not use, and an override button is not real human judgment.

149
00:11:31.969 --> 00:11:33.475
You have to observe the practice.

150
00:11:34.575 --> 00:11:41.423
A good explanation is selective, contrastive, and dialogic, and it is adapted to its audience:

151
00:11:41.423 --> 00:11:43.550
experts, laypeople, or regulators.

152
00:11:43.900 --> 00:11:49.500
We explain to describe, to explain, to justify, and to give grounds to contest.

153
00:11:50.200 --> 00:11:54.599
Contestation can be individual or collective. Individually,

154
00:11:54.599 --> 00:11:58.350
you get an explanation or a justification of your case.

155
00:11:58.700 --> 00:12:03.200
Collectively, you get public statistics, deliberation, NGOs,

156
00:12:03.200 --> 00:12:06.850
and independent authorities like the Défenseur des droits.

157
00:12:07.550 --> 00:12:12.339
Why both? Because individual appeals put the burden on each person,

158
00:12:12.339 --> 00:12:17.100
and they don’t fix the information asymmetry. Collective channels rebalance power.

159
00:12:18.200 --> 00:12:22.253
Now let’s connect the five lectures. I use five storylines,

160
00:12:22.253 --> 00:12:25.750
and they are also on the concept roadmap of the website.

161
00:12:26.100 --> 00:12:32.138
The framing storyline goes from framing, to sociotechnical systems, to the arenas,

162
00:12:32.138 --> 00:12:37.550
to the COMPAS metric, to fair versus radical AI, and ends with the mulching lesson.

163
00:12:38.250 --> 00:12:44.729
The human-in-the-loop storyline: hidden labour, trainers and verifiers, human oversight,

164
00:12:44.729 --> 00:12:48.225
SCHUFA, Article 22, decoupling, and finally contestation.

165
00:12:48.925 --> 00:12:55.372
From ethics to law: charters, the operationalisation gap, soft law, the AI Act pyramid,

166
00:12:55.372 --> 00:12:58.525
high-risk duties, standards, and impact assessments.

167
00:12:59.225 --> 00:13:04.881
The power storyline, and the generative AI storyline, link materiality, labour,

168
00:13:04.881 --> 00:13:07.825
copyright and the general-purpose model rules.

169
00:13:08.175 --> 00:13:13.350
Each storyline is a ready-made long answer: three steps make one argument.

170
00:13:14.450 --> 00:13:20.763
The exam has ten questions: six long ones, one A4 page each, and four short ones,

171
00:13:20.763 --> 00:13:22.050
on one page together.

172
00:13:22.400 --> 00:13:29.544
A long answer has five blocks. First, frame: who asks, what system, what purpose, your framing,

173
00:13:29.544 --> 00:13:31.025
and the value you prioritise.

174
00:13:31.725 --> 00:13:36.821
Then three arguments. Each one goes: claim, course concept with its author,

175
00:13:36.821 --> 00:13:42.575
how it works in this case, and a concrete measure. Take each argument from a different lecture.

176
00:13:43.275 --> 00:13:48.647
Finish with a recommendation: the decision, who is responsible, when to stop,

177
00:13:48.647 --> 00:13:50.250
and the trade-off of your choice.

178
00:13:50.950 --> 00:13:57.035
Here is an example block for a videosurveillance question. It uses the SCHUFA idea,

179
00:13:57.035 --> 00:14:01.800
makes it concrete for police operators, and proposes an audit of override rates.

180
00:14:02.900 --> 00:14:08.000
Short answers take a quarter page. Three moves: define, name, example.

181
00:14:08.700 --> 00:14:15.979
For example, three methods to analyse a sociotechnical system: ethnography, discourse analysis,

182
00:14:15.979 --> 00:14:18.650
and computational methods, with one example.

183
00:14:19.350 --> 00:14:25.538
The teachers said it themselves: for these questions, the names alone earn the points.

184
00:14:25.538 --> 00:14:27.100
So do the four short questions first.

185
00:14:28.200 --> 00:14:34.308
Question 1. A bank adviser signs every loan refusal suggested by the score.

186
00:14:34.308 --> 00:14:36.075
Is the decision automated?

187
00:14:36.425 --> 00:14:38.550
Pause the video and answer.

188
00:14:41.900 --> 00:14:48.133
It can be: under SCHUFA, if the adviser relies decisively on the score,

189
00:14:48.133 --> 00:14:51.950
it is an automated decision and Article 22 GDPR applies.

190
00:14:53.050 --> 00:14:57.250
Question 2. Name the three roles of humans behind AI.

191
00:14:57.600 --> 00:14:59.725
Pause the video and answer.

192
00:15:03.075 --> 00:15:08.151
Trainer (prepares data), verifier (checks outputs),

193
00:15:08.151 --> 00:15:12.425
imitator (pretends to be the AI) — Tubaro et al. 2020.

194
00:15:13.525 --> 00:15:18.400
Question 3. A company signed an ethics charter. Is it legally bound?

195
00:15:18.750 --> 00:15:20.875
Pause the video and answer.

196
00:15:24.225 --> 00:15:30.875
No — charters are soft law. But they can harden: public commitments can be enforced,

197
00:15:30.875 --> 00:15:31.675
and law can absorb them.

198
00:15:32.775 --> 00:15:36.775
Question 4. What lesson does the mulching proposal teach?

199
00:15:37.125 --> 00:15:39.250
Pause the video and answer.

200
00:15:42.600 --> 00:15:43.342
Fairness,

201
00:15:43.342 --> 00:15:50.075
accountability and transparency do not make a system acceptable if its purpose is unacceptable.

202
00:15:51.175 --> 00:15:57.125
Question 5. Emotion recognition to monitor employees: which AI Act level?

203
00:15:57.475 --> 00:15:59.600
Pause the video and answer.

204
00:16:02.950 --> 00:16:08.075
Prohibited (Article 5), except for medical or safety reasons.

205
00:16:09.175 --> 00:16:12.700
To finish, one sentence to carry into the exam.

206
00:16:13.050 --> 00:16:18.625
Frame it. Name the people. Find the rule. Propose a safeguard. And admit the trade-off.

207
00:16:18.975 --> 00:16:20.425
Good luck.
